#!/bin/bash
# truenas-plugin-lvm-filter -- add or remove an LVM global_filter
# entry that prevents LVM from scanning TrueNAS-served NVMe/TCP
# namespaces on the Proxmox host. Addresses GitHub issue #4.
#
# WHY:
#   A guest VM whose disk lives on a TN NVMe namespace often has its
#   own LVM inside (Ubuntu, RHEL and their derivatives default to
#   LVM). The host's LVM scanner sees the guest's PV signatures at
#   the outer namespace level and treats them as host-level PVs.
#   With many namespaces and many VMs cloned from the same template,
#   this produces duplicate-VG warnings on every LVM invocation
#   (pvesm, pvesh, qm, pvestatd polls), and can slow storage-list
#   API calls past the 596s API timeout -- which in turn breaks
#   external tools such as Veeam's backup/restore scans.
#
#   The fix is to teach LVM's global_filter to reject the block
#   devices that come from TrueNAS. We match by /dev/disk/by-id/
#   symlinks whose name starts with `nvme-TrueNAS_`. Those symlinks
#   are populated by udev from the NVMe controller MODEL string
#   (e.g. `TrueNAS PowerEdge R730xd`, `TrueNAS FREENAS-MINI-2.0`)
#   -- local NVMe drives never match, so LOCAL LVM is unaffected.
#
# WHICH FILE:
#   /etc/lvm/lvmlocal.conf is read AFTER /etc/lvm/lvm.conf and
#   OVERRIDES it. On PVE hosts the effective global_filter usually
#   lives in a "truenasplugin managed" devices { } block in
#   lvmlocal.conf. Editing lvm.conf alone would be silently
#   overridden, so this tool targets the EFFECTIVE file: lvmlocal.conf
#   when it carries a global_filter line, else lvm.conf. It detects
#   whether the filter is already active via `lvm dumpconfig`, so it
#   stays idempotent regardless of which file the filter lives in.
#
# WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES:
#   --install    Append the reject regex to the effective global_filter
#                list (lvmlocal.conf preferred, else lvm.conf).
#                Timestamped backup first. Idempotent (no-op if the
#                filter is already active in the effective config).
#   --uninstall  Remove only the regex we added; leave the rest of the
#                global_filter alone. Timestamped backup first.
#   --status     Report where the filter lives and whether it is active.
#   --help       Show this help.
#
# WHY THIS ISN'T DONE AT PACKAGE INSTALL:
#   /etc/lvm/lvm.conf is a conffile owned by the `pve-manager`
#   package and /etc/lvm/lvmlocal.conf is operator-owned. Debian
#   policy discourages one package silently editing another's conffile,
#   and doing so at postinst time would surprise operators who did not
#   consent to a system-wide LVM config change. This script exists so
#   the sysadmin can opt in (or out) explicitly, with a clear audit
#   trail (backup file per invocation) and a clean reversal path.
#
# The right long-term home for this fix is pve-manager itself, in
# the same default global_filter that already excludes ZFS zvols
# and Ceph rbds. Filing an upstream patch is queued as separate
# follow-up work.

set -eu

LVM_CONF=/etc/lvm/lvm.conf
LVM_LOCAL_CONF=/etc/lvm/lvmlocal.conf
# Marker used to identify our-inserted regex on uninstall. The regex
# itself is already unique enough that a plain string match on it is
# safe, but keeping a stable marker gives operators grepping the
# file a clear "yes, this is from us" signal.
OUR_REGEX='"r|/dev/disk/by-id/nvme-TrueNAS_.*|"'
MARKER_TAG='truenas-proxmox-plugin issue #4'

usage() { sed -n '2,57p' "$0"; }

require_root() {
    if [ "$(id -u)" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo "must be run as root" >&2; exit 1
    fi
}

require_lvm_conf() {
    [ -f "$LVM_CONF" ] || [ -f "$LVM_LOCAL_CONF" ] \
        || { echo "no LVM config found ($LVM_CONF / $LVM_LOCAL_CONF) -- is LVM installed?" >&2; exit 2; }
}

# Echo the file whose global_filter line is in effect. lvmlocal.conf is
# read after (and overrides) lvm.conf, so it wins when it carries a
# global_filter line; otherwise lvm.conf. Empty + non-zero if neither.
target_conf() {
    local f
    for f in "$LVM_LOCAL_CONF" "$LVM_CONF"; do
        if [ -f "$f" ] && grep -qE "^[[:space:]]*global_filter[[:space:]]*=" "$f"; then
            echo "$f"; return 0
        fi
    done
    return 1
}

# True if the filter is ALREADY ACTIVE in the effective LVM config,
# regardless of which file it lives in or whether it is anchored (^...).
is_effective() {
    command -v lvm >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
    lvm dumpconfig 2>/dev/null | awk '/global_filter/ && /r\|[^"]*nvme-TrueNAS_/{f=1} END{exit f?0:1}'
}

# True if our regex+marker are present in the given file.
file_has_ours() {
    local f="$1"
    [ -f "$f" ] || return 1
    grep -F "$OUR_REGEX" "$f" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        && grep -F "$MARKER_TAG" "$f" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

show_status() {
    local t
    t=$(target_conf 2>/dev/null || true)
    if [ -n "$t" ]; then
        echo "-- effective global_filter source: $t --"
        grep -nE "^[[:space:]]*global_filter[[:space:]]*=" "$t" || echo "  (no global_filter line)"
    else
        echo "-- no active global_filter line in $LVM_LOCAL_CONF or $LVM_CONF --"
    fi
    echo
    if is_effective; then
        echo "TrueNAS NVMe filter: ACTIVE in the effective LVM config"
    else
        echo "TrueNAS NVMe filter: NOT active"
    fi
    if file_has_ours "$LVM_LOCAL_CONF"; then
        echo "  (our marker found in $LVM_LOCAL_CONF)"
    fi
    if file_has_ours "$LVM_CONF"; then
        echo "  (our marker found in $LVM_CONF)"
    fi
}

backup() {
    local b="$1.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S).truenas-plugin"
    cp -a "$1" "$b"
    echo "backup: $b"
}

do_install() {
    if is_effective; then
        echo "TrueNAS NVMe filter is already active. Nothing to do."
        show_status
        return 0
    fi
    local t
    if ! t=$(target_conf); then
        echo "No active global_filter line in $LVM_LOCAL_CONF or $LVM_CONF." >&2
        echo "Cowardly refusing to create one because that could override" >&2
        echo "defaults set by pve-manager. Add manually inside a devices { ... } section:" >&2
        echo "    global_filter = [ $OUR_REGEX ]   # $MARKER_TAG" >&2
        exit 3
    fi

    backup "$t"
    # Insert our regex before the closing "]" of the array on the
    # global_filter line. Handle both "[a,b,c]" and "[ a, b, c ]"
    # spacings. Add a same-line comment carrying the marker tag.
    # Pass regex + marker as perl -s vars so double-quotes inside
    # the regex don't have to be shell-escaped into a perl literal.
    perl -i -spe '
        if (/^(\s*global_filter\s*=\s*\[)(.*)(\])(\s*)$/) {
            my ($lead, $body, $close, $tail) = ($1, $2, $3, $4);
            $body =~ s/\s+$//;
            $body .= "," unless $body =~ /,\s*$/ || $body =~ /^\s*$/;
            $_ = "${lead}${body} ${r} ${close} # ${m}\n";
        }
    ' -- -r="$OUR_REGEX" -m="$MARKER_TAG" "$t"

    # The perl edit is a silent no-op (exit 0) when the global_filter array
    # spans multiple lines or the closing ] carries a trailing comment. The
    # parse check below cannot catch that because nothing changed -- verify
    # the marker actually landed (it is absent here: idempotency ran first).
    if ! grep -qF "$MARKER_TAG" "$t"; then
        echo "ERROR: edit not applied -- global_filter is multi-line or has a" >&2
        echo "trailing comment after ]. File unchanged (backup kept). Collapse" >&2
        echo "the array to one line and re-run." >&2
        exit 5
    fi

    # Sanity-check the merged config still parses (lvm dumps config on
    # stdout; exits non-zero on parse error).
    if ! lvm dumpconfig >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "WARNING: LVM config appears to no longer parse. Restore backup:" >&2
        echo "  cp $t.bak.*.truenas-plugin $t" >&2
        exit 4
    fi

    echo "Installed in $t. Refresh LVM's device cache with:"
    echo "  pvscan --cache"
    echo "  vgscan --cache"
    echo "  systemctl restart lvm2-monitor"
    show_status
}

do_uninstall() {
    # Find where our marker lives (lvmlocal.conf preferred, else lvm.conf).
    local t=""
    if file_has_ours "$LVM_LOCAL_CONF"; then
        t="$LVM_LOCAL_CONF"
    elif file_has_ours "$LVM_CONF"; then
        t="$LVM_CONF"
    fi
    if [ -z "$t" ]; then
        echo "TrueNAS NVMe filter is not installed by this plugin. Nothing to do."
        return 0
    fi
    backup "$t"
    # Remove our regex (plus any preceding comma or whitespace) and
    # the tail comment marker. Keep the rest of the list intact.
    # Pass regex + marker via perl -s so double-quotes need no
    # shell-escape and \Q...\E works over $r without interpolation
    # of the literal string as a pattern.
    perl -i -spe '
        if (/^(\s*global_filter\s*=\s*\[)(.*)(\])(.*)$/) {
            my ($lead, $body, $close, $tail) = ($1, $2, $3, $4);
            $body =~ s/,\s*\Q$r\E\s*//g;
            $body =~ s/\Q$r\E\s*,\s*//g;
            $body =~ s/\Q$r\E\s*//g;
            # normalise stray inner whitespace/comma left after removal
            $body =~ s/^\s+//; $body =~ s/\s+$//;
            $body =~ s/,\s*,/,/g;
            $tail =~ s/\s*#\s*\Q$m\E\s*$//;
            $_ = "${lead}${body}${close}${tail}\n";
        }
    ' -- -r="$OUR_REGEX" -m="$MARKER_TAG" "$t"

    if ! lvm dumpconfig >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "WARNING: LVM config appears to no longer parse. Restore backup:" >&2
        echo "  cp $t.bak.*.truenas-plugin $t" >&2
        exit 4
    fi

    echo "Removed from $t. Refresh LVM's device cache with:"
    echo "  pvscan --cache"
    echo "  vgscan --cache"
    echo "  systemctl restart lvm2-monitor"
    show_status
}

case "${1:-}" in
    --install)   require_root; require_lvm_conf; do_install ;;
    --uninstall) require_root; require_lvm_conf; do_uninstall ;;
    --status)                  require_lvm_conf; show_status ;;
    -h|--help)   usage ;;
    *)           usage; exit 1 ;;
esac
